      "final_message": "I rewrote `solution.py` so `parse_duration` follows the README. Both public tests pass.\n\n- **How it works:** the whole string has to match, in order, an optional `<digits>h`, then `<digits>m`, then `<digits>s`. The result is `h*3600 + m*60 + s`.\n- **Invalid input:** empty strings and anything that doesn't match raise `ValueError`. Non-string input raises `TypeError`.\n- **Digits:** the pattern accepts only ASCII digits `[0-9]`, not the wider `\\d`, so non-ASCII numerals like `\u0661` are rejected. Leading zeros are fine: `05m` gives 300.\n\nBeyond the public tests, I tried a few more cases:\n- `05m`, `2s`, `1h` and `1h2s` returned the right values.\n- These were all rejected with `ValueError`: wrong order (`1m1h`), an unknown unit (`1d`), a unit with no number (`h`), a trailing space or newline, `+1s`, `-1s`, `\u0661s`, and the empty string.\n- A non-string argument raised `TypeError`.\n\nDONE: Rewrote parse_duration to match the whole string against an ordered h/m/s regex using ASCII digits, return total seconds, and raise ValueError/TypeError for bad input; all public tests pass.",
